WEBVTT

00:00:20.646 --> 00:00:22.773
<i>Just 50 years ago,</i>

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.276
<i>we finally ventured to the moon.</i>

00:00:40.332 --> 00:00:45.796
<i>For the very first time,</i>
<i>we looked back at our own planet.</i>

00:00:53.345 --> 00:00:58.684
<i>Since then, the human population</i>
<i>has more than doubled.</i>

00:01:04.189 --> 00:01:08.610
<i>This series will celebrate</i>
<i>the natural wonders that remain</i>

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.281
<i>and reveal what we must preserve</i>

00:01:12.656 --> 00:01:16.868
<i>to ensure people and nature thrive.</i>

00:01:27.963 --> 00:01:29.965
[animals whooping]

00:01:34.553 --> 00:01:36.805
<i>This is a tropical forest.</i>

00:01:38.181 --> 00:01:39.600
<i>A rain forest.</i>

00:01:40.142 --> 00:01:42.311
<i>The richest habitat on Earth.</i>

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:48.900
<i>Exactly how many species</i>
<i>rain forests contain is unknown,</i>

00:01:49.568 --> 00:01:51.528
<i>but it runs into millions.</i>

00:01:52.738 --> 00:01:54.948
<i>And new ones are discovered every week.</i>

00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:05.417
<i>There are some, like this clouded leopard,</i>
<i>we still know virtually nothing about.</i>

00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:19.222
<i>Although they cover just seven percent</i>
<i>of the world's land area,</i>

00:02:19.598 --> 00:02:23.727
<i>jungles play a vital role</i>
<i>in the health of the planet.</i>

00:02:42.204 --> 00:02:45.457
<i>In the heart of Africa lies the Congo.</i>

00:02:49.252 --> 00:02:53.924
<i>It is our planet's youngest rain forest,</i>
<i>a mere 18,000 years old,</i>

00:02:54.466 --> 00:02:58.261
<i>and it's the jungle</i>
<i>with more big animals than any other.</i>

00:03:17.322 --> 00:03:19.950
<i>A family of lowland gorillas.</i>

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:27.165
<i>They're led by this silverback,</i>
<i>the dominant male.</i>

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:36.049
<i>He's as tall as a man,</i>
<i>but twice the weight.</i>

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:40.220
<i>His family depend on him for survival.</i>

00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:47.519
<i>As their guardian,</i>
<i>he must keep a lookout for danger.</i>

00:03:57.154 --> 00:04:01.116
<i>A rival male might usurp him</i>
<i>and tear his family apart.</i>

00:04:03.827 --> 00:04:07.706
<i>But there is one threat</i>
<i>beyond his control.</i>

00:04:10.167 --> 00:04:11.585
<i>In the last 20 years,</i>

00:04:11.835 --> 00:04:16.089
<i>poaching for bushmeat has halved</i>
<i>the Congo's gorilla population.</i>

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.147
<i>His kind are now critically endangered.</i>

00:04:38.737 --> 00:04:40.155
[grunts]

00:04:45.869 --> 00:04:47.245
[elephant huffs]

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:53.168
<i>It's only a forest elephant,</i>
<i>which needn't concern the silverback.</i>

00:04:57.923 --> 00:04:59.800
[low trumpeting]

00:05:01.051 --> 00:05:04.888
<i>But the elephant</i>
<i>has reason to be wary of poachers too.</i>

00:05:08.475 --> 00:05:11.645
<i>Their long straight tusks</i>
<i>are even more sought after</i>

00:05:11.728 --> 00:05:14.022
<i>than those of their grassland cousins.</i>

00:05:22.113 --> 00:05:23.406
<i>He follows paths</i>

00:05:23.573 --> 00:05:26.326
<i>made by generations of elephants</i>
<i>before him,</i>

00:05:26.785 --> 00:05:28.119
<i>searching for food.</i>

00:05:34.376 --> 00:05:38.922
<i>Like the elephant, the silverback's family</i>
<i>will travel several kilometers a day,</i>

00:05:39.047 --> 00:05:41.007
<i>looking for fruits and seeds.</i>

00:05:45.971 --> 00:05:49.599
<i>They and the elephants</i>
<i>are the gardeners of this jungle.</i>

00:05:50.684 --> 00:05:52.269
<i>The seed dispersers.</i>

00:05:54.521 --> 00:05:56.273
<i>And many of the trees here</i>

00:05:56.356 --> 00:05:59.234
<i>owe their existence</i>
<i>to these large animals.</i>

00:06:05.949 --> 00:06:10.704
<i>Here,</i>
<i>all paths eventually lead to Mbeli Bai.</i>

00:06:18.587 --> 00:06:22.799
<i>Open areas like this are a unique feature</i>
<i>of the Congo's jungles,</i>

00:06:22.883 --> 00:06:26.553
<i>and there are more than a hundred</i>
<i>dotted throughout the forest.</i>

00:06:30.348 --> 00:06:35.270
<i>Known as </i>bais,<i> these clearings attract</i>
<i>many of the jungle's inhabitants.</i>

00:06:44.321 --> 00:06:45.488
[elephant brays]

00:06:47.699 --> 00:06:50.744
<i>The silverback </i>
<i>cautiously leads the way,</i>

00:06:52.537 --> 00:06:54.539
<i>but his family are close behind.</i>

00:07:20.065 --> 00:07:24.235
<i>The gorillas come to feed</i>
<i>on aquatic plants rich in salt,</i>

00:07:24.694 --> 00:07:27.781
<i>a mineral in short supply</i>
<i>inside the forest,</i>

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:30.325
<i>but crucial to their survival.</i>

00:07:43.588 --> 00:07:47.968
<i>Elephants mine salts from the mud</i>
<i>using their trunks.</i>

00:07:56.226 --> 00:07:58.937
<i>Or just drink the mineral-enriched water.</i>

00:08:06.444 --> 00:08:09.823
<i>The silverback's family</i>
<i>are not the only gorillas here.</i>

00:08:11.366 --> 00:08:14.577
<i>Nearly 200 others also use this </i>bai,

00:08:15.662 --> 00:08:18.748
<i>as well as more than 530 elephants,</i>

00:08:19.457 --> 00:08:22.127
<i>a measure of Mbeli's importance.</i>

00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:33.221
<i>For the elephants,</i>
<i>the </i>bais<i> have another vital role.</i>

00:08:37.142 --> 00:08:38.685
<i>Most of their lives are spent</i>

00:08:38.768 --> 00:08:41.980
<i>roaming the vast forested interior</i>
<i>on their own,</i>

00:08:43.064 --> 00:08:46.443
<i>so it's only here</i>
<i>they get a chance to meet others.</i>

00:08:55.285 --> 00:08:59.873
<i>Unlike most of the Congo's other </i>bais,
<i>Mbeli is well protected,</i>

00:09:00.081 --> 00:09:02.250
<i>making it a refuge from poaching.</i>

00:09:06.629 --> 00:09:10.133
<i>It's only when they leave</i>
<i>that the danger returns.</i>

00:09:14.012 --> 00:09:16.890
<i>And as the Congo's main seed dispersers,</i>

00:09:17.307 --> 00:09:21.352
<i>the loss of its megafauna</i>
<i>could have a far-reaching impact</i>

00:09:21.561 --> 00:09:26.232
<i>on the future of our planet's</i>
<i>second-largest rain forest.</i>

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:37.327
<i>Jungles may look the same,</i>

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:40.955
<i>but each is home</i>
<i>to a unique cast of characters.</i>

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:48.755
<i>New Guinea is the world's largest</i>
<i>jungle-covered island,</i>

00:09:49.214 --> 00:09:51.716
<i>and more than half</i>
<i>of its plants and animals</i>

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:54.177
<i>are found nowhere else on Earth.</i>

00:09:58.807 --> 00:10:02.811
<i>Its turbulent geological past</i>
<i>has shaped this vast island</i>

00:10:02.894 --> 00:10:06.981
<i>into a dramatic landscape</i>
<i>of mountains and valleys.</i>

00:10:11.528 --> 00:10:15.198
<i>A process</i>
<i>that isolated one species from another.</i>

00:10:19.077 --> 00:10:20.537
<i>Separated in this way,</i>

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:24.124
<i>New Guinea's animals</i>
<i>have become truly bizarre.</i>

00:10:27.335 --> 00:10:29.879
<i>The male twelve-wired bird-of-paradise</i>

00:10:30.004 --> 00:10:33.925
<i>is the only bird in the world</i>
<i>that has tail ornaments like this,</i>

00:10:35.552 --> 00:10:39.139
<i>used to tickle</i>
<i>the face of a prospective mate.</i>

00:10:41.975 --> 00:10:43.935
<i>From one crow-like ancestor,</i>

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:48.314
<i>birds-of-paradise have evolved</i>
<i>into 40 different kinds.</i>

00:10:50.525 --> 00:10:53.486
<i>Every corner of this island</i>
<i>has its own version.</i>

00:10:55.864 --> 00:10:59.659
<i>Meet the black sicklebill</i>
<i>of New Guinea's highlands...</i>

00:11:01.161 --> 00:11:02.620
[croaks]

00:11:04.038 --> 00:11:07.876
<i>...able to morph</i>
<i>into some very un-birdlike shapes.</i>

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:21.431
<i>Each male bird-of-paradise</i>
<i>has a unique display for attracting mates,</i>

00:11:22.432 --> 00:11:24.601
<i>and none is more extraordinary</i>

00:11:24.851 --> 00:11:28.062
<i>than the one that takes place</i>
<i>on this stage.</i>

00:11:33.359 --> 00:11:37.739
<i>For the owner, a western parotia,</i>
<i>there's work to be done first.</i>

00:11:41.242 --> 00:11:45.079
<i>Every morning, he clears his court</i>
<i>of the night's fallen debris.</i>

00:11:50.126 --> 00:11:53.880
<i>It's a vital chore</i>
<i>if he wants to attract a mate.</i>

00:11:56.883 --> 00:11:59.844
<i>Females only visit the tidiest courts,</i>

00:12:03.264 --> 00:12:06.559
<i>so one rogue leaf might ruin his chances.</i>

00:12:10.605 --> 00:12:11.773
[squawks]

00:12:16.861 --> 00:12:17.820
<i>Perfect.</i>

00:12:19.239 --> 00:12:21.824
<i>His obsessive housework pays off,</i>

00:12:23.034 --> 00:12:25.536
<i>and a female drops in for a closer look.</i>

00:12:27.288 --> 00:12:29.832
<i>Now is his chance to really impress,</i>

00:12:30.917 --> 00:12:32.293
<i>but it won't be easy.</i>

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:35.588
<i>Females are very fussy,</i>

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:39.133
<i>and she'll expect</i>
<i>his carefully choreographed routine</i>

00:12:39.259 --> 00:12:40.176
<i>to be faultless.</i>

00:12:42.178 --> 00:12:44.138
<i>He opens with a bow.</i>

00:12:46.182 --> 00:12:48.851
<i>Next, his blue eye must flash yellow.</i>

00:12:52.522 --> 00:12:53.982
<i>So far, so good.</i>

00:12:55.942 --> 00:12:58.027
<i>He has all the moves.</i>

00:12:59.862 --> 00:13:01.155
<i>Fancy footwork.</i>

00:13:04.367 --> 00:13:05.910
<i>The whirling dervish.</i>

00:13:12.709 --> 00:13:15.837
<i>The head plume shuffle, with spin.</i>

00:13:18.923 --> 00:13:21.509
<i>Her erect head feathers</i>
<i>and quivering wings</i>

00:13:21.592 --> 00:13:23.511
<i>are a very encouraging sign.</i>

00:13:32.312 --> 00:13:35.690
<i>His sidestep and head bob</i>
<i>look good from any angle,</i>

00:13:37.233 --> 00:13:38.484
<i>but his crowning glory</i>

00:13:38.651 --> 00:13:41.988
<i>can only be appreciated</i>
<i>from her perspective.</i>

00:13:44.407 --> 00:13:45.325
<i>Wait for it.</i>

00:13:47.243 --> 00:13:50.455
<i>There it is.</i>
<i>A flash of his iridescent throat patch.</i>

00:13:53.624 --> 00:13:55.501
<i>Her excitement grows.</i>

00:14:12.685 --> 00:14:14.562
<i>His performance has been a triumph,</i>

00:14:17.523 --> 00:14:19.525
<i>and he wins her approval.</i>

00:14:27.909 --> 00:14:32.246
<i>New Guinea's isolation</i>
<i>has created a unique variety of animals,</i>

00:14:34.165 --> 00:14:37.543
<i>but the age of a forest</i>
<i>has an even greater impact</i>

00:14:37.627 --> 00:14:39.962
<i>on the diversity of life it contains.</i>

00:14:43.674 --> 00:14:46.386
<i>The jungles of Borneo, in Southeast Asia,</i>

00:14:46.677 --> 00:14:50.848
<i>have grown here</i>
<i>for nearly 130 million years,</i>

00:14:51.641 --> 00:14:54.060
<i>making them the oldest on Earth.</i>

00:14:59.649 --> 00:15:03.236
<i>This limestone mountaintop</i>
<i>in Borneo's highlands</i>

00:15:03.694 --> 00:15:05.655
<i>was originally formed under the sea.</i>

00:15:08.241 --> 00:15:12.036
<i>Time and rain</i>
<i>has slowly shaped this jungle fortress</i>

00:15:12.245 --> 00:15:14.747
<i>into these 40-meter towers.</i>

00:15:25.299 --> 00:15:29.262
<i>The great age of this island's jungle</i>
<i>is best illustrated</i>

00:15:29.512 --> 00:15:32.515
<i>by one of the oldest predators on Earth.</i>

00:15:43.067 --> 00:15:44.652
<i>A velvet worm.</i>

00:15:46.070 --> 00:15:49.323
<i>Completely at home</i>
<i>on the humid forest floor.</i>

00:15:53.035 --> 00:15:55.746
<i>They've been here</i>
<i>since the age of the dinosaurs,</i>

00:15:56.038 --> 00:15:59.000
<i>and have remained virtually unchanged.</i>

00:16:05.965 --> 00:16:10.052
<i>It detects its insect prey</i>
<i>through vibration and touch.</i>

00:16:11.804 --> 00:16:13.598
<i>But with no turn of speed,</i>

00:16:14.015 --> 00:16:17.018
<i>how does it capture</i>
<i>these fleet-footed critters?</i>

00:16:20.688 --> 00:16:22.899
<i>The answer is stranger than fiction.</i>

00:16:28.779 --> 00:16:30.072
<i>Glue guns.</i>

00:16:34.368 --> 00:16:36.412
<i>The sticky slime hardens on impact,</i>

00:16:38.247 --> 00:16:40.124
<i>trapping the luckless insect.</i>

00:16:41.542 --> 00:16:45.046
<i>The velvet worm will inject the cockroach</i>
<i>with digestive saliva,</i>

00:16:45.922 --> 00:16:48.508
<i>and then suck out its insides.</i>

00:16:55.556 --> 00:17:00.478
<i>The high humidity of the forest floor</i>
<i>isn't just good for velvet worms.</i>

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:08.778
<i>It's perfect for fungi, too,</i>

00:17:10.655 --> 00:17:14.367
<i>and Borneo's ancient jungles</i>
<i>are rich in species.</i>

00:17:26.963 --> 00:17:28.798
<i>By decomposing the dead,</i>

00:17:29.423 --> 00:17:34.136
<i>the slime molds and fungi</i>
<i>recycle scarce nutrients.</i>

00:17:50.528 --> 00:17:55.950
<i>Time has made Borneo's jungles</i>
<i>extraordinarily rich and diverse.</i>

00:17:57.785 --> 00:18:00.454
<i>Here, in just a few hectares of forest,</i>

00:18:00.913 --> 00:18:04.917
<i>there may be more kinds of plants</i>
<i>than in the whole of Europe,</i>

00:18:08.629 --> 00:18:12.425
<i>including</i>
<i>some of the most specialized on Earth.</i>

00:18:18.848 --> 00:18:20.016
<i>Over several weeks,</i>

00:18:20.099 --> 00:18:25.146
<i>these growing tips inflate</i>
<i>to form jugs or pitchers.</i>

00:18:34.655 --> 00:18:38.075
<i>There are 39 kinds of pitcher plant</i>
<i>in Borneo,</i>

00:18:38.576 --> 00:18:41.078
<i>and most are found nowhere else.</i>

00:18:44.373 --> 00:18:49.754
<i>To get the nutrients they need to survive,</i>
<i>pitcher plants rely on animals,</i>

00:18:50.504 --> 00:18:52.548
<i>often in surprising ways.</i>

00:18:57.345 --> 00:19:02.266
<i>This is the mountain treeshrew,</i>
<i>an animal found only in Borneo.</i>

00:19:12.151 --> 00:19:15.780
<i>Every morning, the treeshrew visits</i>
<i>his neighborhood pitchers.</i>

00:19:17.114 --> 00:19:19.241
<i>It depends on the sugary solution</i>

00:19:19.367 --> 00:19:22.453
<i>that exudes</i>
<i>from hairs on the pitcher's lid.</i>

00:19:25.456 --> 00:19:28.751
<i>When he's licked this lid dry,</i>
<i>he'll move to another.</i>

00:19:31.712 --> 00:19:33.422
<i>And then another.</i>

00:19:38.636 --> 00:19:42.723
<i>Eventually, all that sugar</i>
<i>can have only one result.</i>

00:19:44.934 --> 00:19:45.935
[splat]

00:19:50.272 --> 00:19:54.568
<i>In return for the free food,</i>
<i>the treeshrew leaves a gift.</i>

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:58.072
<i>Fertilizer for the plant.</i>

00:20:00.491 --> 00:20:03.369
<i>All that's needed is an afternoon shower</i>

00:20:03.452 --> 00:20:07.164
<i>to flush the shrew poo</i>
<i>into the pitcher's bowl.</i>

00:20:13.295 --> 00:20:17.091
<i>Without these added nitrates,</i>
<i>the plant couldn't survive.</i>

00:20:19.385 --> 00:20:24.432
<i>And the quest for nutrients has led</i>
<i>some pitchers down a sinister path.</i>

00:20:28.894 --> 00:20:30.855
<i>A gracilis pitcher plant</i>

00:20:31.230 --> 00:20:35.735
<i>attracts ants to the underside of its lid</i>
<i>with drops of nectar.</i>

00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:40.656
<i>But what do the ants give in return?</i>

00:20:43.909 --> 00:20:47.288
<i>It's a story that also depends on rain.</i>

00:20:50.082 --> 00:20:51.625
<i>The force of the raindrops</i>

00:20:51.709 --> 00:20:54.378
<i>make it impossible</i>
<i>for the ants to hold on.</i>

00:21:01.177 --> 00:21:05.222
<i>It's the plant world's</i>
<i>only springboard trap.</i>

00:21:06.891 --> 00:21:10.561
<i>This pitcher plant</i>
<i>feeds on ant bodies.</i>

00:21:18.444 --> 00:21:21.614
<i>All pitcher plants</i>
<i>depend on animals for survival,</i>

00:21:23.282 --> 00:21:26.869
<i>and one has even gone into business</i>
<i>with a bat.</i>

00:21:29.497 --> 00:21:32.625
<i>The woolly bat finds hemsleyana pitchers</i>

00:21:32.875 --> 00:21:38.339
<i>because the plant's broad back wall</i>
<i>reflects the bat's ultrasound.</i>

00:21:44.303 --> 00:21:48.349
<i>The tube-like pitchers</i>
<i>are the perfect roosting site.</i>

00:21:52.436 --> 00:21:55.231
<i>The pitcher provides secure accommodation</i>

00:21:55.689 --> 00:21:59.652
<i>and the plant is enriched</i>
<i>by its guest's deposits.</i>

00:22:04.073 --> 00:22:07.868
<i>Relationships like these</i>
<i>have taken millions of years to evolve,</i>

00:22:08.369 --> 00:22:11.247
<i>but many could be gone in a decade.</i>

00:22:14.917 --> 00:22:20.005
<i>In the last 50 years,</i>
<i>Borneo has lost over half of its jungle.</i>

00:22:23.551 --> 00:22:25.261
<i>And it's even worse</i>

00:22:25.344 --> 00:22:27.972
<i>on the neighboring islands</i>
<i>of the Philippines.</i>

00:22:30.307 --> 00:22:34.228
<i>Here, 90 percent </i>
<i>of the primary rain forest has gone.</i>

00:22:41.068 --> 00:22:46.490
<i>What's left is the last refuge</i>
<i>for the world's rarest bird of prey.</i>

00:22:47.616 --> 00:22:49.827
<i>The mighty Philippines eagle.</i>

00:22:55.374 --> 00:22:56.208
[caws]

00:22:56.959 --> 00:22:59.628
<i>With just 400 pairs remaining,</i>

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:03.173
<i>a chick on a nest is a very scarce sight.</i>

00:23:07.344 --> 00:23:10.472
<i>Scientists have tracked the mother</i>
<i>since she was a year old.</i>

00:23:11.223 --> 00:23:14.643
<i>Now aged ten, this is her first chick.</i>

00:23:22.318 --> 00:23:26.030
<i>It's four months old,</i>
<i>already nearly a meter tall,</i>

00:23:26.822 --> 00:23:28.741
<i>and increasingly confident.</i>

00:23:31.493 --> 00:23:34.997
<i>Although Mother still likes to give</i>
<i>a helping hand.</i>

00:23:42.671 --> 00:23:45.215
<i>The chick is very demanding.</i>

00:23:48.802 --> 00:23:51.347
<i>She expects regular food drops,</i>

00:23:52.097 --> 00:23:56.936
<i>but, like a toddler having a tantrum,</i>
<i>she doesn't want any help at mealtimes.</i>

00:23:57.061 --> 00:23:58.646
[cawing]

00:24:01.273 --> 00:24:04.026
<i>Already, she's the same size as her father</i>

00:24:04.485 --> 00:24:06.528
<i>and too big to argue with.</i>

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:11.283
<i>But if his youngster</i>
<i>is to take her next steps,</i>

00:24:11.533 --> 00:24:14.745
<i>he knows it's time for some tough love.</i>

00:24:16.205 --> 00:24:19.249
<i>From now on,</i>
<i>visits will be less frequent.</i>

00:24:29.551 --> 00:24:30.803
[caws]

00:24:30.928 --> 00:24:34.765
<i>The parents drop by</i>
<i>with an occasional food parcel,</i>

00:24:37.643 --> 00:24:41.105
<i>but a small fruit bat</i>
<i>isn't much to get excited about.</i>

00:24:48.696 --> 00:24:49.780
<i>It's confusing</i>

00:24:49.863 --> 00:24:53.951
<i>when your parents don't visit</i>
<i>as frequently as they once did.</i>

00:25:04.003 --> 00:25:04.837
[caws]

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:06.630
<i>Every day, she calls,</i>

00:25:08.298 --> 00:25:10.384
<i>but no one is paying any attention.</i>

00:25:15.639 --> 00:25:16.473
<i>She's hungry,</i>

00:25:17.975 --> 00:25:20.352
<i>and all that's left are scraps.</i>

00:25:26.275 --> 00:25:29.236
<i>It's time to become a proper eagle.</i>

00:25:34.241 --> 00:25:38.454
<i>Flapping strengthens flight muscles</i>
<i>in two-meter wings.</i>

00:25:43.125 --> 00:25:46.587
<i>Wings that will be used</i>
<i>to carry off her hefty prey.</i>

00:25:51.842 --> 00:25:54.803
<i>Though that skill is still some time off.</i>

00:25:59.975 --> 00:26:03.729
<i>So far, she's never ventured</i>
<i>beyond the security of her nest.</i>

00:26:08.984 --> 00:26:10.486
<i>And with good reason.</i>

00:26:15.532 --> 00:26:18.869
<i>There's a 70-meter drop</i>
<i>to the forest floor.</i>

00:26:24.083 --> 00:26:25.667
<i>A fall would be fatal.</i>

00:26:32.966 --> 00:26:37.054
<i>It's a nervous moment,</i>
<i>but she must persevere.</i>

00:26:39.556 --> 00:26:42.351
<i>Right now, she's all toes and talons.</i>

00:26:52.569 --> 00:26:58.325
<i>Sudden gusts of wind are not helpful</i>
<i>when balancing over a death-defying drop,</i>

00:27:00.953 --> 00:27:03.705
<i>and the daily downpours</i>
<i>don't make it any easier.</i>

00:27:14.258 --> 00:27:18.846
<i>Every day, she gets a little stronger</i>
<i>and more confident.</i>

00:27:25.102 --> 00:27:30.399
<i>Then, the day comes when confidence</i>
<i>finally matches know-how.</i>

00:27:48.250 --> 00:27:51.503
<i>Now is the moment to reach for the sky.</i>

00:28:00.470 --> 00:28:03.515
<i>Her first ever flight from her nest tree.</i>

00:28:07.227 --> 00:28:10.981
<i>It'll be nearly a year</i>
<i>before she's fully independent.</i>

00:28:13.066 --> 00:28:16.320
<i>Then, her survival</i>
<i>will depend on finding a territory</i>

00:28:16.904 --> 00:28:19.781
<i>with large stretches of unspoilt jungle.</i>

00:28:26.496 --> 00:28:28.457
<i>In the Philippines' fragmented forest,</i>

00:28:28.665 --> 00:28:32.669
<i>there's just too little prey</i>
<i>for a supersized eagle.</i>

00:28:36.757 --> 00:28:40.719
<i>But there is one jungle</i>
<i>whose size is still legendary.</i>

00:28:45.098 --> 00:28:49.144
<i>The Amazon basin</i>
<i>is over 3,000 kilometers across</i>

00:28:49.478 --> 00:28:53.732
<i>and home to half of our planet's</i>
<i>remaining rain forest.</i>

00:29:00.697 --> 00:29:04.743
<i>But there are challenges for those</i>
<i>living in the Amazon's deep interior.</i>

00:29:05.077 --> 00:29:06.662
[birds hoot and chirp]

00:29:14.378 --> 00:29:16.088
<i>Black spider monkeys,</i>

00:29:17.631 --> 00:29:19.841
<i>with eyes on a salt lick.</i>

00:29:23.887 --> 00:29:27.933
<i>Being so far from the sea,</i>
<i>salt is in short supply here,</i>

00:29:28.684 --> 00:29:31.353
<i>so a visit to a lick is essential.</i>

00:29:36.191 --> 00:29:40.237
<i>But first, they must be sure</i>
<i>there are no predators lurking below.</i>

00:29:42.864 --> 00:29:45.450
<i>A harmless agouti is a good sign.</i>

00:29:54.334 --> 00:29:58.338
<i>This is the only time a spider monkey</i>
<i>ever sets foot on the ground.</i>

00:30:04.052 --> 00:30:05.887
<i>And if you have your head in a hole,</i>

00:30:07.597 --> 00:30:08.974
<i>best to have a lookout.</i>

00:30:11.727 --> 00:30:14.604
<i>Monkeys can't survive</i>
<i>without these minerals,</i>

00:30:15.856 --> 00:30:17.524
<i>and predators know it.</i>

00:30:21.403 --> 00:30:22.863
[chitters]

00:30:22.946 --> 00:30:24.865
<i>A warning from the lookout.</i>

00:30:35.500 --> 00:30:37.169
<i>It's not a false alarm.</i>

00:30:41.548 --> 00:30:42.507
<i>A jaguar.</i>

00:30:44.676 --> 00:30:47.846
<i>Rarely seen,</i>
<i>but the Amazon's top predator.</i>

00:30:56.104 --> 00:30:59.608
<i>With the jaguar gone, the monkeys return,</i>

00:31:01.026 --> 00:31:02.527
<i>but it's a risk.</i>

00:31:03.195 --> 00:31:06.156
<i>Nobody knows</i>
<i>when the predator will be back.</i>

00:31:13.705 --> 00:31:18.126
<i>It's not just spider monkeys</i>
<i>for whom fear must give way to need.</i>

00:31:19.795 --> 00:31:23.965
<i>In the Amazon, salt licks are as vital</i>
<i>as an oasis in a desert.</i>

00:31:31.098 --> 00:31:33.183
<i>Noises could be friend or foe,</i>

00:31:33.266 --> 00:31:36.937
<i>so every visitor</i>
<i>must remain poised for flight.</i>

00:31:50.242 --> 00:31:51.243
[rustling]

00:31:54.079 --> 00:31:55.622
[cawing]

00:32:00.460 --> 00:32:01.962
<i>It's a false alarm.</i>

00:32:02.337 --> 00:32:04.506
<i>Just the harmless agouti again.</i>

00:32:10.095 --> 00:32:14.558
<i>In the Amazon, every animal group</i>
<i>has more species than anywhere else.</i>

00:32:17.644 --> 00:32:19.479
<i>And that includes frogs.</i>

00:32:22.941 --> 00:32:26.611
<i>There are thought to be over a thousand</i>
<i>different kinds of them here,</i>

00:32:30.615 --> 00:32:33.493
<i>and new ones are still being discovered.</i>

00:32:49.801 --> 00:32:52.971
<i>Frogs are an indicator</i>
<i>of a healthy forest,</i>

00:32:54.347 --> 00:32:59.728
<i>but its most important residents</i>
<i>are the jungle's smallest inhabitants.</i>

00:33:16.953 --> 00:33:21.833
<i>There are thought to be over two million</i>
<i>kinds of insect in the Amazon.</i>

00:33:23.502 --> 00:33:25.670
<i>These are the most numerous.</i>

00:33:30.842 --> 00:33:35.055
<i>A colony of leafcutter ants</i>
<i>can run into the millions.</i>

00:33:41.311 --> 00:33:45.357
<i>Each colony is a superorganism</i>
<i>with a collective function...</i>

00:33:46.608 --> 00:33:51.071
<i>to gather leaves and carry them back</i>
<i>to their underground nests.</i>

00:34:13.176 --> 00:34:15.595
<i>Within the territory</i>
<i>of a leafcutter colony,</i>

00:34:15.679 --> 00:34:21.685
<i>as much as a fifth of all new plant growth</i>
<i>will be harvested by the ants.</i>

00:34:23.520 --> 00:34:26.565
<i>Slicing through leaves</i>
<i>with specialized mandibles,</i>

00:34:26.856 --> 00:34:32.070
<i>they produce high frequency vibrations,</i>
<i>which stiffens the leaf,</i>

00:34:32.904 --> 00:34:34.281
<i>making it easier to cut.</i>

00:34:36.491 --> 00:34:41.162
<i>Working together,</i>
<i>they can strip a tree bare in 24 hours.</i>

00:34:52.090 --> 00:34:56.386
<i>The trick is holding on to your leaf</i>
<i>as you fall.</i>

00:35:01.141 --> 00:35:02.892
<i>Easier said than done.</i>

00:35:04.644 --> 00:35:08.023
<i>But best not to return to the nest</i>
<i>empty-handed.</i>

00:35:15.655 --> 00:35:19.075
<i>A leafcutter colony </i>
<i>is like a mega-herbivore,</i>

00:35:19.326 --> 00:35:21.828
<i>not dissimilar</i>
<i>to an elephant in the Congo,</i>

00:35:21.911 --> 00:35:25.999
<i>and their impact on the ecosystem</i>
<i>is just as important.</i>

00:35:28.710 --> 00:35:32.047
<i>The ants follow scent trails</i>
<i>back to the nest,</i>

00:35:35.383 --> 00:35:37.552
<i>dodging unexpected obstacles.</i>

00:35:42.057 --> 00:35:47.062
<i>But to increase efficiency,</i>
<i>the ants keep their paths clear of debris.</i>

00:36:04.496 --> 00:36:08.083
<i>A leafcutter nest</i>
<i>can be up to eight meters deep</i>

00:36:08.291 --> 00:36:11.419
<i>and have thousands</i>
<i>of interconnected chambers.</i>

00:36:15.090 --> 00:36:18.134
<i>The workers</i>
<i>carry the freshly harvested leaves</i>

00:36:18.343 --> 00:36:20.845
<i>to special rooms for processing.</i>

00:36:23.848 --> 00:36:28.687
<i>The leaves are not food,</i>
<i>but used to create gardens of fungus,</i>

00:36:28.770 --> 00:36:30.647
<i>which they feed to their larvae.</i>

00:36:35.694 --> 00:36:40.824
<i>The success of the colony depends on</i>
<i>keeping their gardens disease-free.</i>

00:36:42.033 --> 00:36:46.788
<i>So, the ants have teamed up</i>
<i>with bacteria to help control pathogens,</i>

00:36:46.955 --> 00:36:49.249
<i>which would destroy their precious fungi.</i>

00:36:53.378 --> 00:36:54.838
<i>Scientists now believe</i>

00:36:54.921 --> 00:36:59.300
<i>these bacteria could provide</i>
<i>new solutions to human diseases.</i>

00:37:03.221 --> 00:37:05.473
<i>But as successful as these ants are,</i>

00:37:05.682 --> 00:37:11.604
<i>the rain forest's diversity depends on</i>
<i>no one species gaining the upper hand.</i>

00:37:14.441 --> 00:37:15.817
[animal hoots]

00:37:18.903 --> 00:37:21.573
<i>Here,</i>
<i>ants don't have it all their own way.</i>

00:37:25.034 --> 00:37:27.245
<i>This one is behaving oddly.</i>

00:37:32.709 --> 00:37:35.336
<i>It seems compelled to climb upwards,</i>

00:37:35.754 --> 00:37:39.048
<i>and is already</i>
<i>several meters above the forest floor.</i>

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:43.845
<i>Something has taken control</i>
<i>of its movements,</i>

00:37:44.179 --> 00:37:47.432
<i>like a puppeteer</i>
<i>pulling at the strings of a marionette.</i>

00:37:54.063 --> 00:37:58.943
<i>There's just one final act,</i>
<i>for which the ant has no choice.</i>

00:38:03.656 --> 00:38:08.995
<i>It must find a place to bite down,</i>
<i>tethering it to the vegetation.</i>

00:38:24.344 --> 00:38:26.763
<i>With the ant in its death grip,</i>

00:38:27.180 --> 00:38:31.518
<i>a parasitic fungus, Cordyceps,</i>
<i>erupts from its body.</i>

00:38:41.486 --> 00:38:46.241
<i>Finally, the fruiting body of the fungus</i>
<i>bursts from its head.</i>

00:39:01.172 --> 00:39:05.844
<i>From this bulbous container,</i>
<i>spores will be cast into the air currents,</i>

00:39:05.927 --> 00:39:09.055
<i>where they will claim more ant victims.</i>

00:39:13.476 --> 00:39:15.061
<i>But it's not just ants.</i>

00:39:15.854 --> 00:39:19.315
<i>Many others are infected</i>
<i>by the Cordyceps fungus.</i>

00:39:38.459 --> 00:39:40.545
<i>The more numerous a species is,</i>

00:39:40.628 --> 00:39:44.966
<i>the more likely it is to fall victim</i>
<i>to the killer fungus.</i>

00:39:52.390 --> 00:39:57.270
<i>Checks and balances like these</i>
<i>means no one species can ever dominate,</i>

00:39:58.271 --> 00:40:01.482
<i>so protecting</i>
<i>the jungle's incredible diversity.</i>

00:40:15.121 --> 00:40:19.417
<i>But today,</i>
<i>the diversity of the world's rain forests</i>

00:40:19.626 --> 00:40:22.253
<i>is falling at an alarming rate,</i>

00:40:23.129 --> 00:40:25.006
<i>and that is because of us.</i>

00:40:29.093 --> 00:40:34.974
<i>We have now replaced</i>
<i>up to 27 million hectares of virgin jungle</i>

00:40:35.350 --> 00:40:37.810
<i>with a single species of tree.</i>

00:40:42.398 --> 00:40:44.233
<i>This is oil palm,</i>

00:40:44.901 --> 00:40:47.195
<i>one of the world's most productive crops.</i>

00:40:48.571 --> 00:40:49.864
<i>But these monocultures</i>

00:40:49.948 --> 00:40:55.495
<i>support only a fraction of the diversity</i>
<i>found in primary rain forests,</i>

00:40:56.371 --> 00:40:59.540
<i>and it is pushing many animals</i>
<i>to extinction.</i>

00:41:04.003 --> 00:41:07.507
<i>In the swamp forests of northern Sumatra,</i>
<i>in Indonesia,</i>

00:41:07.882 --> 00:41:11.761
<i>time is running out</i>
<i>for one of our closest ancestors.</i>

00:41:14.305 --> 00:41:15.598
<i>The orangutan.</i>

00:41:21.062 --> 00:41:25.858
<i>A baby orangutan takes more than ten years</i>
<i>to become independent.</i>

00:41:28.319 --> 00:41:30.655
<i>Everything must be learned</i>
<i>from its mother.</i>

00:41:31.739 --> 00:41:34.993
<i>Like how to bridge the gaps between trees.</i>

00:41:40.832 --> 00:41:43.292
<i>Survival depends on each orang</i>

00:41:43.376 --> 00:41:46.462
<i>understanding its patch of forest</i>
<i>perfectly.</i>

00:41:49.382 --> 00:41:53.636
<i>Watching scientists</i>
<i>have named this pair Ellie and Eden.</i>

00:41:56.347 --> 00:41:57.974
<i>Ellie is 18 years old,</i>

00:41:59.976 --> 00:42:02.770
<i>and Eden, her first baby, is three.</i>

00:42:07.608 --> 00:42:11.404
<i>Right now,</i>
<i>he needs a little bit of encouragement.</i>

00:42:20.830 --> 00:42:22.373
<i>Like all orangutans,</i>

00:42:22.832 --> 00:42:25.585
<i>Ellie has a mental map</i>
<i>of her surroundings,</i>

00:42:26.377 --> 00:42:29.839
<i>including the location</i>
<i>of every fruiting tree.</i>

00:42:34.969 --> 00:42:37.138
<i>Eden needs to learn this, too,</i>

00:42:37.722 --> 00:42:41.517
<i>but for now,</i>
<i>he must watch everything his mother does,</i>

00:42:42.977 --> 00:42:47.690
<i>learning not just what's edible</i>
<i>but also how to eat it.</i>

00:42:58.659 --> 00:43:02.038
<i>Every day is a new lesson for Eden.</i>

00:43:06.125 --> 00:43:11.130
<i>Here, ants are a big part of their diet,</i>
<i>and there's a knack to gathering them.</i>

00:43:15.718 --> 00:43:19.472
<i>Once tangled in Ellie's fur,</i>
<i>they're easy to pick off.</i>

00:43:23.976 --> 00:43:28.314
<i>For little Eden,</i>
<i>it's all about watching and learning.</i>

00:43:35.029 --> 00:43:38.741
<i>Eden's cousin, Louis, is more experienced.</i>

00:43:40.201 --> 00:43:41.577
<i>Four years older than Eden,</i>

00:43:42.120 --> 00:43:44.455
<i>he can move through the branches</i>
<i>with ease.</i>

00:43:55.216 --> 00:43:57.885
<i>It's taken a few years to master,</i>

00:43:58.803 --> 00:44:02.473
<i>and it's clearly a skill</i>
<i>he feels is worth showing off.</i>

00:44:13.276 --> 00:44:18.698
<i>Conquering the treetops is just stage one</i>
<i>in an orangutan's education.</i>

00:44:19.198 --> 00:44:22.160
<i>To survive, Louis must learn more.</i>

00:44:24.871 --> 00:44:26.038
<i>Much more.</i>

00:44:29.417 --> 00:44:32.336
<i>His mother, Lisa,</i>
<i>has found a rotten tree trunk</i>

00:44:32.920 --> 00:44:34.630
<i>with lots of ants.</i>

00:44:35.840 --> 00:44:40.052
<i>But this anting opportunity</i>
<i>requires more know-how.</i>

00:44:41.512 --> 00:44:44.974
<i>To get them, Lisa must break sections off.</i>

00:44:50.980 --> 00:44:53.900
<i>At his age, Louis must do more than watch.</i>

00:44:56.861 --> 00:44:57.945
<i>He must copy.</i>

00:45:16.005 --> 00:45:17.006
<i>Not a bad effort.</i>

00:45:21.886 --> 00:45:26.974
<i>But even at the age of seven,</i>
<i>he's yet to master the art of ant capture.</i>

00:45:33.105 --> 00:45:35.107
<i>Louis has much to learn.</i>

00:45:39.654 --> 00:45:44.075
<i>Pluto, on the other hand,</i>
<i>graduated a long time ago.</i>

00:45:46.118 --> 00:45:48.829
<i>Thirty years old</i>
<i>and nearly a hundred kilograms,</i>

00:45:49.163 --> 00:45:51.123
<i>he is king of this jungle.</i>

00:45:54.043 --> 00:45:57.129
<i>His cheek flanges are a sign of power,</i>

00:45:58.172 --> 00:46:00.258
<i>something for Louis to aspire to.</i>

00:46:06.013 --> 00:46:08.975
<i>Pluto is constantly</i>
<i>on the lookout for food,</i>

00:46:10.726 --> 00:46:13.729
<i>and he's just spotted</i>
<i>a promising opportunity.</i>

00:46:16.774 --> 00:46:19.860
<i>These wasps mean one thing to him.</i>

00:46:21.654 --> 00:46:23.155
<i>Tasty grubs.</i>

00:46:28.411 --> 00:46:33.124
<i>To get at them,</i>
<i>he favors a smash-and-grab technique.</i>

00:46:39.255 --> 00:46:43.301
<i>His thick fur protects him</i>
<i>from most of the angry wasps,</i>

00:46:43.801 --> 00:46:46.762
<i>but the rewards</i>
<i>are worth risking a few stings.</i>

00:46:52.435 --> 00:46:54.353
<i>Louis keeps his distance.</i>

00:46:57.982 --> 00:47:00.609
<i>He would need to be</i>
<i>a lot bigger and hairier</i>

00:47:00.776 --> 00:47:02.820
<i>before he could do battle with wasps.</i>

00:47:10.328 --> 00:47:15.124
<i>But here, there's one trick that can take </i>
<i>half a lifetime to get to grips with.</i>

00:47:22.465 --> 00:47:27.094
<i>Louis' mother, Lisa, has learned</i>
<i>how to make tools from sticks,</i>

00:47:27.470 --> 00:47:29.388
<i>to winkle out insects.</i>

00:47:31.599 --> 00:47:33.142
<i>The orangutans living here</i>

00:47:33.309 --> 00:47:36.729
<i>are the only ones in the world</i>
<i>to have discovered this skill.</i>

00:47:40.358 --> 00:47:42.360
<i>Louis must try and learn it too,</i>

00:47:42.443 --> 00:47:46.113
<i>but it may be another eight years</i>
<i>before he's as good as his mother.</i>

00:47:49.367 --> 00:47:52.370
<i>This long education has made orangutans</i>

00:47:52.453 --> 00:47:56.207
<i>particularly vulnerable</i>
<i>to changes taking place in their forest.</i>

00:48:01.420 --> 00:48:06.342
<i>It's now estimated that we lose</i>
<i>100 orangutans every week</i>

00:48:06.801 --> 00:48:08.469
<i>from human activity.</i>

00:48:17.728 --> 00:48:23.401
<i>Louis and Eden's generation</i>
<i>could be the last wild orangutan.</i>

00:48:28.531 --> 00:48:33.744
<i>When Pluto was their age,</i>
<i>his jungle home stretched to the horizon.</i>

00:48:39.625 --> 00:48:41.085
<i>Not anymore.</i>

00:48:44.338 --> 00:48:46.257
<i>In the last four decades,</i>

00:48:46.674 --> 00:48:50.636
<i>the pristine lowland jungle </i>
<i>that orangutans depend on</i>

00:48:51.220 --> 00:48:55.474
<i>has declined by a staggering 75 percent.</i>

00:48:58.727 --> 00:49:02.189
<i>Across the world,</i>
<i>we are losing tropical forest</i>

00:49:02.440 --> 00:49:07.111
<i>at the rate</i>
<i>of nearly 15 million hectares every year,</i>

